% ============================================================================
% ocean-instrument-secondary-quantity — sonar → the SECOND quantity it
% determines, decoded from a span already sitting unused inside the SAME
% NOAA source `ocean-observing-instruments.adj` already cites
% (adj-facts-stdlib, OCEANOGRAPHY).
% ============================================================================
% A sibling to the already-shipped `ocean-observing-instruments.adj`
% (`ocean_instrument(instrument, quantity)`, ONE quantity per instrument —
% sonar → distance_to_object): a new `ocean_instrument_secondary_quantity
% (instrument, secondary_quantity)` table names a SECOND quantity the SAME
% already-cited NOAA "Sonar" page states sonar determines, decoded from a
% clause already sitting unused inside that table's own header truth
% table -- no new WebFetch. That table's schema has room for only ONE
% quantity per instrument; the already-quoted sonar sentence also carries
% a second, distinct quantity that never made it into a row:
%
%   sonar → orientation_of_object
%     determines "the range and orientation of the object" by measuring
%     the time between an emitted sound pulse and its echo
%
% ("range" is the already-tabled `distance_to_object`; "orientation" is
% the leftover fact this sibling captures.)
%
% Recall is a binding query:
%
%     ? ocean_instrument_secondary_quantity(sonar, $SecondaryQuantity)  % orientation_of_object
%
% This is a native `table` (ADJ-TABLES RS-5, a TWO-column table like the
% parent's own): the single `row` lowers to a ground relation
% `ocean_instrument_secondary_quantity(instrument, secondary_quantity)`
% carrying the citation, so the lookup above is the ordinary SLD binding
% query -- the engine returns the quantity WITH its source, on the CPU,
% with zero model calls, and abstains on any instrument that is not the
% one tabled here (it never invents a second quantity). The query also
% runs BACKWARD -- bind a quantity and recall which instrument determines
% it:
%
%     ? ocean_instrument_secondary_quantity($Instrument, orientation_of_object)  % sonar
%
% This is honestly a ONE-ROW table, not a forced three-row one: neither
% the tide_gauge nor the hydrophone quote already sitting inside the
% parent table's own header names a SECOND quantity for that instrument
% -- tide_gauge's cited span states only sea level relative to a datum,
% and hydrophone's cited span ("detects and records ocean sounds from all
% directions") names a direction-independence characteristic, not a
% distinct second MEASURED quantity -- so this table correctly abstains
% on both rather than inventing one.
%
% Provenance: reproduces, byte-for-byte, the SAME sonar span already
% quoted inside `ocean-observing-instruments.adj`'s own header/provenance
% section, from the SAME National Oceanic and Atmospheric Administration
% (NOAA) "Sonar" page that table already cites -- no new WebFetch, only a
% different schema decoding a part of that already-verified quote. `trust
% authoritative` -- the same tier the parent table already carries for
% this row (a primary U.S. government .gov source).
% (See ../README.md; feedback_nothing_human_authored,
% feedback_adj_only_shipped_artifacts.)
% ============================================================================

table ocean_instrument_secondary_quantity {
    columns instrument, secondary_quantity

    row (sonar, orientation_of_object)

    source "the range and orientation of the object"
    locator "https://oceanservice.noaa.gov/facts/sonar.html"
    trust authoritative
}
